{"entries":[{"entry_key":"CLM_1","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","claim":"The safest reclamation protocol is a generation-tracked consensus lease protocol requiring proof-of-absence before reclaiming any workspace associated with a generation; the protocol must durably record the count and identity of completed evidence nodes per generation.","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_1"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_2","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","claim":"The safest protocol uses a content-addressed Merkle-DAG where completed evidence is an ancestral node and reclamation only garbage-collects orphaned leaves; silent displacement is structurally impossible because overwriting a content-addressed hash changes its address.","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_2"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_3","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","claim":"The safest protocol is deterministic replay from the last durable barrier, treating a workspace without a terminal record as unverified and requiring replay-to-barrier before reclamation; the protocol must durably record barrier epoch markers, deterministic state digests at each barrier, and the content hash of the completed evidence set.","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_3"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_4","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","claim":"If the workspace is strictly deterministic and oracle-free, the safest protocol is trivial because any displacement is detectable by recomputation; the only necessary durable record is a certificate that the workspace boundary contains no hidden non-deterministic inputs, or if oracles exist, their exact values must be durably recorded.","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_4"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_5","claim_class":"conflict","claim":"The available formal artifacts advance conflicting, mutually incompatible designs for the safest reclamation protocol and disagree on what must be durably recorded.","premise_keys":["CLM_1","CLM_2","CLM_3","CLM_4"],"conflict_handles":["ART_1","ART_2","ART_3","ART_4"],"source_conflict_keys":["CNF_1"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_6","claim_class":"unknown","claim":"Which reclamation protocol is safest and guaranteed never to silently displace completed evidence cannot be determined from the bounded record.","premise_keys":["CLM_5"],"source_conflict_keys":["CNF_1"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_7","claim_class":"unknown","claim":"What exactly the safest protocol must durably record cannot be determined from the bounded record.","premise_keys":["CLM_5"],"source_conflict_keys":["CNF_1"]}],"source_conflicts":[{"conflict_key":"CNF_1","conflicting_handles":["ART_1","ART_2","ART_3","ART_4"],"description":"The supplied formal artifacts assert incompatible, mutually exclusive designs as the unique safest reclamation protocol and specify different required durable records."}],"uncovered_requirements":[{"requirement":"External evidence or a formal comparative safety proof adjudicating which of the proposed reclamation protocols guarantees never to silently displace completed evidence.","related_entry_keys":["CLM_6"]},{"requirement":"System-model constraints establishing which protocol prerequisites are satisfied, such as content-addressed storage availability, consensus lease infrastructure, deterministic replay capability, or oracle-free guarantees.","related_entry_keys":["CLM_7"]}]}